# Ω_{F/K} is free of rank one over a separable extension of K(x)

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.kaehlerRankOne_of_transcendental`
(`4dc3da55-8bb5-5803-9131-85f94c90f6f4`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `e3b8f68c-c606-5a58-93e4-cb393c668caa`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be an element transcendental
over $K$, i.e. not algebraic over $K$. Assume that $F$ is a separable algebraic extension of the
intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$ (the hypothesis
`Algebra.IsSeparable (IntermediateField.adjoin K ({x} : Set F)) F`, which includes that every
element of $F$ is algebraic over $K(x)$ with separable minimal polynomial). The conclusion is the
conjunction of two assertions about the module $\Omega_{F/K}$ of Kähler differentials of $F$ over
$K$, regarded as an $F$-module: it is a free $F$-module, and its rank `Module.finrank F Ω[F⁄K]`
equals $1$. No finiteness hypothesis on the extension $F/K(x)$ is imposed, and no basis is exhibited
in the statement itself (the assertion is freeness together with the numerical value of the rank).

This is the standard computation that a function field in one variable which is separably generated
has a one-dimensional space of Kähler differentials, $\Omega_{F/K} = F\,dx$. It feeds the
construction of curves over a field in this development, being cited by
[`AlgebraicCurve.isCurveOver_of_transcendental`]
(thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.isCurveOver_of_transcendental)
and by
[`AlgebraicCurve.exists_isCurveOver_adjoin_range_eq_top_apply_algEquiv_eq_pow_of_pow_eq_one`]
(thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.
exists_isCurveOver_adjoin_range_eq_top_apply_algEquiv_eq_pow_of_pow_eq_one).

